Solution: First, we find the expression for $d_{n}$, the greatest common divisor of $a_{n}$ and $a_{n+1}$, where $a_{n}=100+n^{2}$.

\begin{aligned} d_{n} &=\operatorname{gcd}\{100+n^{2}, 100+(n+1)^{2}\} \\ &=\operatorname{gcd}\{100+n^{2}, 100+(n+1)^{2}-(100+n^{2}\} \\ &=\operatorname{gcd}\{100+n^{2}, 100+2n+1-100-n^{2}\} \\ &=\operatorname{gcd}\{100+n^{2}, 2n+1\} \end{aligned}

Since $2n+1$ is odd, let $2n+1=K$, then $0<100+n^{2}=K\left(n-\frac{1}{2}\right)+\frac{101}{2}=M K+\frac{101}{2}$, where $-K<M<K$. When $M=0$, $101$ is an integer, then

\begin{aligned} d_{n} &=\operatorname{gcd}\{100+n^{2}, 2n+1\} \\ &=2n+1 \\ & \text { When } M=0, 2 n+1=101, \text { but } a_{n} \text { and } a_{n+1}, \text { are not divisible by } 2n+1 \\ {\therefore} d_{n} &=\left.2n+1=\operatorname{gcd}\left\{100+n^{2}, 100+(n+1)^{2}\right\} \\ &=101 . \end{aligned}

In this way, the answer is $\operatorname{gcd}\{100+n^{2}, 100+(n+1)^{2}\}=101$.

